     <article class="story single" data-beat="health">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">HEALTH</span> · ET Healthworld · 2026-10-23 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Centre Directs States to Ban Reuse of Single-Use Medical Devices</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Health</span> <span>#Medical Devices</span> <span>#Regulation</span> <span>#Patient Safety</span></div>
       <p>The Centre has issued a directive to states, mandating the enforcement of a ban on the reuse of single-use medical devices. This significant move aims to uphold patient safety standards across the healthcare system.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-23/centre-directs-states-to-ban-reuse-of-single-use-medical-dev.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The directive follows an investigation by the Directorate General of Health Services (DGHS). The DGHS found that specific private and corporate hospitals were engaged in the practice of sterilising these products and subsequently using them on multiple patients.</p><p>Such devices are explicitly designated for single use only, making their re-sterilisation and reuse a violation of established medical protocols. The Centre's instruction seeks to halt this practice, ensuring adherence to prescribed safety guidelines.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft is clear, concise, and accurately reflects the provided summary and the necessity for the directive.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://health.economictimes.indiatimes.com/news/policy/centre-directs-states-to-enforce-ban-on-reuse-of-single-use-medical-devices/135142674" rel="noopener">ET Healthworld</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
