     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · BBC Science · 2026-10-23 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Danish Nurdles Linked to River Tyne Spill</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#science</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>Nurdles found along the Danish coast in September likely originated from a major plastic pellet spill in north-east England in August. The Danish Environment Protection Agency confirmed that tests carried out alongside Aarhus University suggest the microplastics match the characteristics of the one billion pellets spilled in the River Tyne. Current patterns and circulation in the North Sea support their arrival in Denmark during late September and early October.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-23/danish-nurdles-linked-to-river-tyne-spill.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Startup founder Signe Simonsen first spotted the milk-white plastic pellets on the island of Mandø on September 26. Simonsen collected samples from 10 different locations along the Danish coast and Sylt in Germany before handing them to the environment agency. Authorities noted there were no other recorded spills in the area to explain the presence of the pellets.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief is accurate, concise, and captures the essential details from the source regarding the nurdle spill investigation.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cqy43xv1jwv2o?at_medium=RSS&amp;at_campaign=rss" rel="noopener">BBC Science</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
