     <article class="story single" data-beat="business">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">BUSINESS</span> · Economic Times · 2026-10-23 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>India Ends Asian Para Games with Record 65 Medals</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Para Games</span> <span>#India</span> <span>#Athletics</span> <span>#Medals</span></div>
       <p>India concluded its para-athletics campaign at the Asian Para Games in Nagoya with a record-shattering haul of 65 medals. This achievement surpasses the previous edition's total in Hangzhou by 12 medals, where India secured 53 medals, including 16 gold, 17 silver, and 20 bronze. The Indian para-athletics team, comprising 62 members, finished with an impressive tally of 22 gold, 19 silver, and 24 bronze. This performance positioned India as the top-ranked nation after China, which led with 98 medals (54 gold, 24 silver, 20 bronze). Uzbekistan secured 64 medals (20 gold, 21 silver, 23 bronze), while host nation Japan clinched 51 medals (19 gold, 18 silver, 14 bronze).</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-23/india-ends-asian-para-games-with-record-65-medals.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>On the final day of competitions, India added 13 medals to its tally, comprising 4 gold, 3 silver, and 6 bronze. Among the gold medalists was two-time Paralympic champion javelin thrower Sumit Antil, who successfully defended his title in the men's F42-44/61-64 combined event. Antil, also the reigning world champion and world record holder, achieved a gold-winning throw of 72.46m in the F64 classification. Pranav Soorma secured gold in the men's club throw F51, defending his previous edition's title with a Games record of 32.87m, having also won silver at the 2024 Paris Paralympics. Sandeep clocked a world record 52.28 seconds to win the men's 400m T64 final, finishing 0.85 seconds ahead of Saudi Arabia's Nour Alsana. Haney also set a world record in the men's javelin throw F37/38 with a throw of 60.49m, earning 1116 Raza points.</p><p>Other medalists on the final day included Parmod, who won silver in the men's 1500m T46, clocking 4:09.65. Mahendra Gurjar secured a silver in the men's javelin throw F42-F44/F61-F64 with 59.03m, and Nimisha Chakkungalparambil claimed silver in the women's long jump T47 with a best effort of 5.37m. Bronze medals were earned by Sandeep (men's 100m T44), Rakeshbhai Bhatt (men's 200m T37), Ajay Kumar (men's 400m T64), Navdeep Singh (men's Javelin Throw F40/F41), Dharamvir (men's club throw F51), and Sandip Sanjay Sargar (men's Javelin Throw F42-F44/F61-F64).</p><p>Sumit Antil's journey to success is notable; born in Khewra, Haryana, he lost his father at age seven. Initially aspiring to a wrestling career, a road accident in 2015 at 17 years old resulted in the amputation of his left leg. The competition also featured combined events like F42, F44, F62, and F64 for discus or javelin throw, categorizing athletes with lower-limb deficiencies based on prosthesis use. F51 athletes compete in seated positions due to extensive movement impairment, while T47 and T46 classifications denote athletes with unilateral upper-limb impairments, showcasing the diverse range of athletic challenges and achievements at the Games.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft is comprehensive, well-structured, and includes the specific medal counts and athlete details required for a thorough report.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://economictimes.indiatimes.com/news/sports/other-sports/india-end-para-athletics-campaign-with-record-65-medals-in-asian-para-games/articleshow/135149001.cms" rel="noopener">Economic Times</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
