     <article class="story single" data-beat="sports">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SPORTS</span> · NDTV Sports · 2026-10-23 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>India Surpasses 100 Medals, Shatters Three World Records at Asian Para Games 2026</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Asian Para Games</span> <span>#India</span> <span>#Para Sports</span> <span>#Aichi-Nagoya</span></div>
       <p>India's contingent at the Asian Para Games 2026 in Aichi-Nagoya breached the 100-medal mark on Day 5, propelled by three new world records. Mona Agarwal, Sandeep Kumar, and Haney each set new global benchmarks in their respective disciplines, contributing to India's significant medal haul. This surge in performance underscored a dominant day for the Indian athletes.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-23/india-surpasses-100-medals-shatters-three-world-records-at-a.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Mona Agarwal secured her second gold medal of the Games in the women's 50m rifle 3 positions SH1, setting a new world record with a score of 357.5. She outscored China's Cuiping Zhang by a margin of 3.8 points. Earlier, Agarwal had triumphed in the women's 10m air rifle standing SH1 event. In athletics, Sandeep Kumar claimed gold and a T44 world record with a sprint time of 52.28s in the men's 400m, surpassing former record holder Nour Alsana of Saudi Arabia. Haney also set an F37 world record in the javelin throw, achieving a massive 60.49m on his final attempt, adding to his earlier discus throw bronze. Haney had previously broken his own world record of 59.72m with this throw.</p><p>The gold rush extended to other events. Nitesh Kumar defeated Pramod Bhagat in an all-Indian men's singles SL3 badminton final to secure gold, with Bhagat taking silver. Harvinder Singh and Vijay Sundi clinched the top spot in the para archery men's recurve team event, overcoming China with a 5-1 victory. In badminton, Naveen Sivakumar won gold in the men's singles SL4, beating Indonesia's Ferdy Setiawan 21-9, 21-17. Supporting these gold victories, Ajay Kumar secured a bronze in the men's 400m T44 with a time of 53.61s, joining Sandeep Kumar on the podium and setting a new Games record.</p><p>Further medal contributions included Nithya Sre, who earned a silver in the women's singles SH6 badminton, falling to China's Fengmei Li 12-21, 13-21 in 26 minutes. Nimisha Chakkungalparambil also secured silver in the women's long jump T47 with a best effort of 5.37m, finishing behind Sri Lanka's Dhananjana, who set a new Games record of 5.52m. Notably, Avani Lekhara finished fourth in the women's 50m rifle 3 positions SH1, missing a podium spot after strong contention in the prone position. India's performance on Day 5 at Aichi-Nagoya solidified its strong presence at the Asian Para Games 2026.</p><p>What to watch: The Asian Para Games 2026 continue in Aichi-Nagoya with more events.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft effectively captures the key achievements and events from the source material, including specific world records and medal outcomes.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://sports.ndtv.com/othersports/asian-para-games-2026-day-5-live-updates-india-medal-tally-results-aichi-nagoya-live-sheetal-devi-12220955#publisher=newsstand" rel="noopener">NDTV Sports</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
