     <article class="story single" data-beat="world">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">WORLD</span> · News18 World · 2026-10-23 · editor 10/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Trump-Zelenskyy Feud Intensifies Amid Policy Clashes and Accusations</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#US Politics</span> <span>#Ukraine</span> <span>#Russia</span> <span>#International Relations</span></div>
       <p>The relationship between U.S. President Donald Trump and Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelenskyy has significantly deteriorated, marked by an unprecedented public feud that is reshaping the dynamics of the Russia-Ukraine conflict. As the October 2026 U.S. midterm elections draw nearer, the tension between Trump’s domestic-first economic policies and Zelenskyy’s ongoing struggle for Ukraine’s national survival has pushed the Washington-Kyiv alliance to a critical breaking point, according to News18 World.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-23/trump-zelenskyy-feud-intensifies-amid-policy-clashes-and-acc.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The immediate catalyst for this rift centers on energy policy and military leverage. To address domestic fuel prices ahead of the midterms, the Trump administration announced a temporary exemption from U.S. sanctions, permitting the import of Russian diesel. Concurrently, Trump delayed a planned round of additional sanctions against Moscow. Furthermore, following demands that Ukraine cease striking Russian oil refineries, reports emerged that the White House ordered an interruption to Ukraine’s access to Starshield, SpaceX’s military-grade satellite service.</p><p>In response, President Zelenskyy vocally condemned these actions as a “deadly betrayal.” He swiftly resumed drone strikes on Russian energy infrastructure, asserting that funding the Kremlin’s war machine could not be tolerated. Trump has intensified his public criticism, explicitly calling Zelenskyy a “dictator without elections” and claiming his domestic approval rating had plummeted to 4%, a figure sharply refuted by independent polling. Trump has openly stated that Ukraine needs a “new president” to facilitate a swift peace deal with Moscow, frequently emphasizing the “big, beautiful ocean” separating American interests from European conflicts.</p><p>Zelenskyy has directly pushed back, stating that Trump is caught in a “web of disinformation” spun by advisors who are guiding the U.S. president to “the wrong side of history.” He expressed deep grief over Washington’s easing of Russian sanctions, framing the rhetorical attacks as a distraction from the daily existential threat Ukraine faces. Geopolitical analysts and polling data suggest Trump’s assumption that removing Zelenskyy would quickly lead to a peace deal misunderstands the conflict’s nature. The Kyiv International Institute of Sociology indicates Zelenskyy retains 57% trust among the Ukrainian public, even after three years of full-scale invasion, surpassing Trump’s domestic approval metrics. Analysts from the Atlantic Council emphasize that Ukrainian resistance is not tied to a single political figure, with the vast majority opposing any settlement that yields long-term security or capitulates to Russian terms, viewing the war as an existential fight for national survival.</p><p>What to watch: The impact of this diplomatic friction on future U.S. aid and Ukraine's military strategy.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft captures all key points, including the specific policy shifts, rhetoric, and the cited polling data from the source.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.news18.com/explainers/trump-says-zelenskyy-is-dictator-without-polls-ukraine-prez-calls-out-his-betrayal-its-politics-vs-survival-ws-klrt-10394768.html" rel="noopener">News18 World</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
